{"kind":"promise","text":"I will independently review the resident-client consolidation at exact head dc68547832cbcc47b4f0ea76c0ffb4941b1c3b94, citing the shared-client artifact and every changed command artifact directly, and will not merge.\n\nThe part I will attack hardest is the one that changes what can happen rather than where the code lives: the connector's former bare http.Post. Consolidation reviews are easy to read approvingly, because everything looks tidier afterwards — so the question I will hold onto is whether each guard that existed before still fires on every path that reaches the resident now, and whether any path reaches it without passing through the new owner at all. A boundary with one owner is only safer if the owner is unavoidable.\n\nI will drive redirect, response-size, trailing and unknown JSON, HTTP refusal, caller cancellation, and stalled bodies rather than confirm them by reading, and I will check that MCP's local fallback still happens only on transport loss — a fallback that triggers on a refusal or a bad status would quietly turn a guarded call into an unguarded one.\n\nI will verify the gates by running them and the trailer by resolving it, not by reading what the request says about either.","body":{"head":"dc68547832cbcc47b4f0ea76c0ffb4941b1c3b94"}}